

00:00:43.563 --> 00:00:49.256
My name's Liz Fuller and I'm a running coach with Compton Park Runners Club.

00:00:49.256 --> 00:01:03.096
Welcome to my podcast. If you're thinking about taking up running, I'm here to help. There are many training programmes available online which aim to help people build up to running 5 kilometers.

00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:12.935
Some of them are great and thousands of people of all ages are taking part in 5 kilometre races across the country as a result.

00:01:12.935 --> 00:01:18.612
People like them because they're easy to follow and don't push them too hard.

00:01:18.612 --> 00:01:30.686
However, they don't work for everyone, especially if you suffer from something like a heart condition or asthma because they're aimed at people with average fitness and running ability.

00:01:30.686 --> 00:01:40.662
Another thing is that everyone is different and if you have any specific questions related to your needs, there's no one to provide any answers.

00:01:40.662 --> 00:01:46.599
I have a couple of simple tips I always give to new runners.

00:01:46.599 --> 00:01:52.102
I expect you've been told to run very slowly until your fitness increases.

00:01:52.102 --> 00:01:55.966
Well, I find that can prevent progress.

00:01:55.966 --> 00:02:04.125
You should run at a speed that feels comfortable, but time yourself and try to run a bit faster each time.

00:02:04.125 --> 00:02:12.138
Listening to music can be very helpful. It takes your mind off things and helps your body get into a rhythm.

00:02:12.138 --> 00:02:21.706
I'd say that is better than running with a friend, especially as most people are competitive and that's not what you want when you're just starting.

00:02:21.706 --> 00:02:30.818
I don't think the time of day is especially important. Some people are better in the evening, while others are morning people.

00:02:30.818 --> 00:02:38.173
But you need to be consistent, so aim to train regularly. Twice a week is enough to begin with.

00:03:24.879 --> 00:03:35.589
New members often say to me that they've been put off running either because they lack confidence or they don't have time or they think they dislike running.

00:03:35.589 --> 00:03:40.678
Kerry, for example, joined the club two years ago at the age of 40.

00:03:40.678 --> 00:03:50.905
She'd always enjoyed running at school but wasn't sure if she'd be able to do it. She was worried about being left behind and being the slowest runner.

00:03:50.905 --> 00:03:57.418
But she says she was made to feel so welcome she soon forgot all about that.

00:03:57.418 --> 00:04:06.924
James had always hated the idea of running but a friend encouraged him to come along for a taster session and he hasn't looked back.

00:04:06.924 --> 00:04:12.510
He never misses a training session despite having a really demanding job.

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:22.816
Leo was worried about having to commit himself to training sessions every week and wasn't sure he'd be able to fit training into his busy schedule.

00:04:22.816 --> 00:04:30.270
But after experiencing a lot of stress at work he came along to us and gave it a go.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:36.350
Now he says he feels much more relaxed and he looks forward to his weekly run.

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:40.651
Mark is quite typical of our new members.

00:04:40.651 --> 00:04:51.207
He's never considered himself to be a sporty person and it was only when he retired that he decided to take up the challenge of trying to run five kilometres.

00:04:51.207 --> 00:05:03.977
It took him months to find the courage to contact us but felt reassured immediately as there were other people his age who were only just taking up running for the first time.

00:05:03.977 --> 00:05:07.777
My own journey hasn't been easy.

00:05:07.777 --> 00:05:20.247
I did my first marathon when I was 37 after having had two kids. My husband had been running marathons for years but I never dreamed I'd be doing one with him.

00:05:20.247 --> 00:05:26.773
I managed to complete it in four hours, but I felt like giving up half way through.

00:05:26.773 --> 00:05:30.796
It was only the support of the spectators that kept me going.

00:05:30.796 --> 00:05:44.273
I do think signing up for a race of whatever length is motivating, whether it's 5k or 25k, because it's good to have something to work towards and it gives you a sense of achievement.

00:05:44.273 --> 00:05:53.169
I did my first 10k after only 6 months, which was certainly very challenging and not something I'd necessarily recommend.

00:05:53.169 --> 00:05:59.141
But after you've been training for a few weeks, it's worth putting your name down for a 5k.

00:05:59.141 --> 00:06:07.841
Some people find they only need a few practice runs before taking part in a race, but I'd give yourself a couple of months at least.

00:06:07.841 --> 00:06:12.479
Well, I hope that's given you a really good idea of what's going on.
